## Explanation
I don't have information about "structs" in the provided reference material. However, I can explain the concept based on general C programming knowledge. 

Think of a `struct` (short for structure) as a custom "container" or a "blueprint" for a complex object. While an **array** (which we discussed earlier) can only hold items of the *same* type, a `struct` allows you to group different types of variables together under one name. Imagine you are building a digital address book; instead of having separate variables for a name, an age, and a phone number, you can create a single `struct` called `Person` that holds all those pieces of information together as one unit.

## Use Cases
You use `structs` when you need to represent real-world entities that have multiple attributes. For example, if you are making a game, you might use a `struct` to represent a `Player`, which needs to store a `char` for their name, an `int` for their health, and a `float` for their position on the map. It makes your code much cleaner and easier to manage because you can pass that entire "package" of data around your program instead of passing ten individual variables.

